Qt for Python (PySide6) 6.11.1, and Qt 6
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Copyright (C) The Qt Company Ltd. and other contributors.
Licence: LGPL-3.0-only, or GPL-2.0-only, or GPL-3.0-only, or a Qt commercial
licence. RapidPDF relies on the LGPL-3.0 option.
https://doc.qt.io/qtforpython/

Verified from the installed distribution metadata of PySide6, PySide6-Essentials,
PySide6-Addons and shiboken6, all 6.11.1, each of which states
"LGPL-3.0-only OR GPL-2.0-only OR GPL-3.0-only". Each also records a
LicenseRef-Qt-Commercial.txt, which is the alternative, not the one in use here.

THE RELINKING OBLIGATION, WHICH A PYINSTALLER BUNDLE IS EXACTLY WHERE PEOPLE
MISS IT. LGPL-3.0 requires that a recipient of the binary be able to replace the
LGPL library with their own modified version and still run the program. In a
onedir PyInstaller bundle the Qt libraries are ordinary DLLs sitting next to the
executable, loaded dynamically at run time, so this is satisfied in practice:
a recipient can drop in a rebuilt Qt DLL of the same version and the app picks
it up. Two build settings that RapidPDF already sets for unrelated reasons keep
it that way, and both must stay:

  - onedir, not onefile. A onefile build unpacks to a temp folder on every
    launch, which makes substitution far harder to argue.
  - upx=False. Packing the DLLs would obscure exactly what is being replaced.

Both are set in rapid-pdf.spec, where the comments explain the antivirus
reasoning. This paragraph is the second reason not to change them. Since
RapidPDF is itself AGPL-3.0 and its complete source is public, the LGPL's other
conditions are met by the same source offer that covers the rest of the app.


